+---
+id: task-001-stable-task-ids
+type: task
+title: "Stable task ids"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Stable task ids
+
+Give each task a unique identifier, so the app can refer to a task in a way that still works after the
+task is renamed.
+
+## What it asks
+
+- `add` returns a task with an `id`: a string that no other task of the list has.
+- `TaskList.get(id)` returns the task with that id, as a `Result<Task>` like the list's other methods;
+  an unknown id fails with `not-found`.
+- `find`, `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag`, `untag` and `remove` accept a task's id as well as its
+  title.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Ids are deterministic.** The domain may not read the clock or make random values
+  (`r3-no-clock-or-randomness`), so the list hands out `task-1`, `task-2`, ... from a counter it keeps.
+  The counter never goes back, so a removed task's id is not reused by the same list.
+- **Id before title.** A reference is first looked up as an id; only when no task has that id is it
+  looked up as a title. An id is the stable reference, so it must never resolve to a different task. The
+  cost: a task whose title equals another task's id cannot be found by that title; it is still found
+  by its own id.
+- **Titles stay unique.** Adding or renaming to a title another task has still fails with `duplicate`.
+  Ids do not replace that rule.
+- **Ids are not renamed.** `rename` changes the title and keeps the id.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- Two tasks added to a list get different ids; the id of a task does not change when it is renamed,
+  completed, reopened, tagged or untagged.
+- `get(id)` returns the task; `get` of an unknown id, or of a removed task's id, fails with `not-found`.
+- Each of the seven title methods works with the id of a task, and with its title.
+- A rename to an existing title still fails with `duplicate`; renaming a task to its own title succeeds.
+- The public types export `Task` with the `id` field.
